WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.930
Silver and gold sit one beneath the other in the periodic table and their electron arrangements are almost identical; one shines white, the other yellow.

2
00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:15.300
Chemistry is not enough to explain that difference — you need the equations Einstein wrote in nineteen oh five.

3
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:23.110
Because gold's innermost electrons are circling at one hundred and sixty thousand kilometres a second, and at that speed they grow heavier.

4
00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:36.400
Picture two rings in a jeweller's window, lying side by side on the same velvet.

5
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:42.450
Both were hammered into the same shape, polished on the same wheel, and sit under the same light.

6
00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:45.300
One is white, one is yellow.

7
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:50.710
We take that difference so utterly for granted from childhood that we never think to ask about it.

8
00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:54.520
And yet chemistry expects these two metals to be near-twins.

9
00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:58.650
On the periodic table, silver and gold sit in the same column.

10
00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:02.460
Copper is with them too, at the top of that same column.

11
00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:11.710
All three have the same arrangement of outer electrons: a completely filled d shell, and above it, standing entirely alone, a single s electron.

12
00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:16.480
The whole power of the periodic table is hidden in exactly that promise.

13
00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:20.130
Same column means similar behaviour.

14
00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:25.700
Drop sodium into water, then potassium, and both froth with the same fury.

15
00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:30.630
Fluorine and chlorine snatch electrons from anything that comes near them.

16
00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:33.480
The logic of the column works.

17
00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:37.290
Gold and silver keep that promise, for the most part.

18
00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:42.060
Both are extraordinarily soft, spreading under a hammer without tearing.

19
00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:49.070
Both conduct electricity superbly; silver is the best conductor known, and gold comes in just behind copper.

20
00:01:49.070 --> 00:01:55.280
Both settle into the same crystal arrangement in the solid state, the face-centred cubic structure.

21
00:01:55.280 --> 00:02:01.650
Their atomic radii are even almost exactly the same, roughly 144 picometres.

22
00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:10.420
That fact alone is strange, because gold sits a full row below silver on the table; it has one extra shell, and it ought to be noticeably fatter.

23
00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:12.150
It is not.

24
00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:22.360
And because far more mass is packed into the same volume, gold is nearly twice as dense as silver: 19.3 grams per cubic centimetre against 10.5.

25
00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:25.050
But one is white and one is yellow.

26
00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:29.020
Let us first look at what the colour of a metal even means.

27
00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:36.510
Metals are lustrous because inside them there is a sea of electrons that belongs to no single atom, roaming freely through the whole mass.

28
00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:46.560
That sea does not care what frequency the light falling on it happens to have; the electrons oscillate along with the incoming wave and immediately radiate the energy back out.

29
00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:50.370
Every colour of visible light is sent back in equal measure.

30
00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:53.220
The result is a colourless brightness.

31
00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:59.110
This is how a mirror works, this is why steel looks grey, this is why silver is white.

32
00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:06.440
For a metal to acquire a colour, that equality has to break — the metal has to selectively swallow part of the visible spectrum.

33
00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:11.370
A little way beneath the sea of free electrons lies a completely filled d band.

34
00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:17.740
An electron down there can leap up and join the sea, but doing so requires a certain threshold of energy.

35
00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:22.510
In silver that threshold is about 4 electronvolts.

36
00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:28.240
Convert it to a wavelength and you get 310 nanometres, which is in the ultraviolet.

37
00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:33.970
In other words, the light silver is capable of absorbing lies in a region our eyes cannot see.

38
00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:38.580
The whole of visible light comes back untouched, and silver shines white.

39
00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:43.670
In gold, the threshold for that same leap is about 2.4 electronvolts.

40
00:03:43.670 --> 00:03:51.480
That means 520 nanometres, and that falls squarely inside visible light, on the border between blue and green.

41
00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:59.930
Gold swallows the violet and blue portion of the light falling on it and sends the rest back — part of the green, the yellow, the orange, the red.

42
00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:02.940
Our eye reads that mixture as a warm yellow.

43
00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:07.710
The yellow of gold is really the absence of the blue that gold cannot give back.

44
00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:10.560
So the question sharpens.

45
00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:19.010
If the outer electron arrangement of the two metals is the same, if their crystal structures are the same, if even their atomic sizes are the same,

46
00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:25.380
then why does that threshold sit in the ultraviolet for one of them and right in the middle of our vision for the other?

47
00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:34.630
The gap between them is more than 1.5 electronvolts; on the atomic scale that is not a deviation to be shrugged off, it is a different world entirely.

48
00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:40.200
Let us set copper aside, because the answer to copper's redness is the easier one.

49
00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:49.610
In copper the d band already sits very close to the conduction sea, the threshold naturally falls into the visible region, and no unusual explanation is needed for it.

50
00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:52.780
Copper is the easy member of the trio.

51
00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:55.310
The anomaly is gold.

52
00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:58.320
And gold's anomaly does not come alone.

53
00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:04.530
Silver slowly tarnishes in air, a dark layer of sulphide forming on its surface.

54
00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:06.900
Gold does not tarnish.

55
00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:14.870
When a gold mask that has waited 3,000 years under the earth is lifted out, it comes out with the surface it had on the day it was buried.

56
00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:21.720
That one of two metals sitting in the same column should be so chemically reluctant is also something that demands explaining.

57
00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:29.210
The answer to these three oddities — the colour, the size, and the reluctance — is not in the chemistry textbooks.

58
00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:34.780
The answer is in a paper written in 1905 on the electrodynamics of moving bodies.

59
00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:40.030
You do not need to look at black holes or satellite orbits to see Einstein.

60
00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:43.040
Looking at the ring on your finger is enough.

61
00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:48.610
In the nucleus of a gold atom there are 79 protons.

62
00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:53.700
That is the number which determines how fierce the pull exerted on the electrons is.

63
00:05:53.700 --> 00:06:03.430
The innermost electron is held right at the bottom of that nucleus, in an extraordinarily narrow region, and a rough calculation can tell us how fast that electron is moving.

64
00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:12.040
In the simple model of the atom, the speed of the innermost electron is found by dividing the number of protons in the nucleus by 137.

65
00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:23.050
That number 137 is not arbitrary; it is the inverse of the fine structure constant, one of nature's fundamental constants, and it sets the strength of the electromagnetic interaction.

66
00:06:23.050 --> 00:06:31.660
In hydrogen, that is to say in a nucleus with a single proton, the electron's speed is one 137th of the speed of light.

67
00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:33.390
Negligible.

68
00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:37.040
But in gold we have 79 protons.

69
00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:42.450
79 divided by 137 comes to roughly 0.58.

70
00:06:42.450 --> 00:06:51.220
Which is to say the innermost electron of a gold atom moves at 58 percent of the speed of light, roughly 175,000 kilometres per second.

71
00:06:51.220 --> 00:07:02.390
A margin of honesty has to be left here: the electron does not really orbit like a planet, it is spread out as a cloud of probability, and assigning it a classical speed is a crude thing to do.

72
00:07:02.390 --> 00:07:10.680
But the order of magnitude of the momentum is right, and full calculations that include relativity from the outset confirm this rough estimate.

73
00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:17.690
The inner electrons of gold have long since entered the region where relativity is no longer a correction but the main effect.

74
00:07:17.690 --> 00:07:20.860
What happens at 58 percent of the speed of light?

75
00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:24.030
This is where special relativity enters.

76
00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:30.720
The inertia of a moving body increases; the electron behaves as though its mass were 23 percent greater.

77
00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:37.890
And since the diameter of the region the electron occupies is inversely proportional to its mass, that region shrinks.

78
00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:44.100
The innermost shell is roughly one fifth smaller than it would be in a universe where relativity were switched off.

79
00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:46.950
It collapses onto the nucleus.

80
00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:50.920
Who cares about the innermost electron, you might say.

81
00:07:50.920 --> 00:07:54.250
It is the outer electron that does the chemistry.

82
00:07:54.250 --> 00:07:59.980
And here is precisely the crux of the story: the contraction does not stay inside.

83
00:07:59.980 --> 00:08:08.270
A strict rule of quantum mechanics requires electron regions of the same type to remain distinct from one another, never blending together.

84
00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:14.800
So when the innermost s region contracts, all the s regions outside it are forced to contract along with it.

85
00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:19.570
In gold, the chain reaches all the way out to the outermost electron.

86
00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:26.100
And that outermost electron is exactly what makes gold gold.

87
00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:32.150
The contraction that relativity sets off at the bottom of the nucleus is carried directly to the valence electron.

88
00:08:32.150 --> 00:08:37.880
That electron is drawn inward, bound more tightly to the nucleus, and its energy drops.

89
00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:41.850
At the same time a second effect works in the opposite direction.

90
00:08:41.850 --> 00:08:48.060
The s and p electrons that have collapsed inward screen the positive charge of the nucleus more effectively.

91
00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:56.990
The d electrons out beyond them never venture down to the bottom of the nucleus, so they are left behind that screen and feel the nucleus more weakly than before.

92
00:08:56.990 --> 00:09:00.800
They loosen, they expand, their energy rises.

93
00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:03.010
Now put the picture together.

94
00:09:03.010 --> 00:09:09.860
In gold, the energy of the lone outer electron goes down, and the energy of the filled d band just beneath it goes up.

95
00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:14.950
The gap between them closes from both sides at once, like a pair of pincers.

96
00:09:14.950 --> 00:09:22.760
Calculations that leave relativity out find that threshold in the ultraviolet for gold, that is, they give a result resembling silver.

97
00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:29.770
Calculations that include relativity bring the threshold down to 2.4 electronvolts and paint gold yellow.

98
00:09:29.770 --> 00:09:33.260
This is not a claim that stayed on paper.

99
00:09:33.260 --> 00:09:44.430
A relativistic band calculation carried out in 1971 reproduced gold's measured reflectance curve properly for the first time; non-relativistic calculations could not manage it.

100
00:09:44.430 --> 00:09:49.840
Switch relativity off in the computer and gold turns the colour of silver.

101
00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:52.690
Switch it on and it goes yellow.

102
00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:56.020
So why does it not happen in silver?

103
00:09:56.020 --> 00:09:59.670
Silver has 47 protons in its nucleus.

104
00:09:59.670 --> 00:10:04.920
47 divided by 137 comes to roughly 0.34.

105
00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:13.690
A third of the speed of light — still an unthinkable speed — but relativistic effects do not depend on speed linearly, they depend on it far more sharply.

106
00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:18.460
In silver the mass increase is not 23 percent but around 6.

107
00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:25.790
The contraction is real, but it is not strong enough to drag the threshold out of the ultraviolet and into visible light.

108
00:10:25.790 --> 00:10:28.000
Silver stays white.

109
00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:32.610
What is more, gold is particularly fortunate in this respect.

110
00:10:32.610 --> 00:10:42.500
Along the sixth row of the periodic table, the relativistic contraction of the outer electron does not simply grow steadily; it peaks at exactly 79 protons.

111
00:10:42.500 --> 00:10:45.830
Chemists call that peak the gold maximum.

112
00:10:45.830 --> 00:10:51.080
Gold sits at the very point where relativity is most visible in chemistry.

113
00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:54.090
And here lies the irony of the story.

114
00:10:54.090 --> 00:11:04.300
Dirac, who wrote down the relativistic equation of quantum mechanics, stated in 1929 that in situations where high speeds do not come into play — that is,

115
00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:11.790
in atomic and molecular structure and in ordinary chemical reactions — relativity would have no appreciable effect.

116
00:11:11.790 --> 00:11:22.320
That view was generally accepted in chemistry for forty years; taking relativity seriously was the business of a minority who concerned themselves with the fine details of heavy nuclei.

117
00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:31.410
One of the works that really pulled the subject together was published in 1979, and it laid out how relativity runs through the entire periodic table.

118
00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:36.820
The counter-example, meanwhile, had been sitting on people's fingers for thousands of years.

119
00:11:36.820 --> 00:11:40.310
The same pincer explains gold's other oddities.

120
00:11:40.310 --> 00:11:51.000
Because the outer electron is bound more tightly, tearing an electron off gold is markedly harder than tearing one off silver: 890 kilojoules per mole against 731.

121
00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:56.090
A metal that reluctant to give up its electron does not readily enter into reactions.

122
00:11:56.090 --> 00:12:03.740
Gold's refusal to tarnish, that stubborn cleanliness which made it money and sacred object throughout history, comes straight from here.

123
00:12:03.740 --> 00:12:09.150
More surprising still, gold is willing to take an electron rather than give one.

124
00:12:09.150 --> 00:12:19.520
When it accepts an electron it gains 2.3 electronvolts; that value is far from anything typical of a metal and close instead to iodine's 3.1 electronvolts.

125
00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:23.970
Which is to say gold can behave chemically almost like a halogen.

126
00:12:23.970 --> 00:12:34.500
Combine it with caesium and what emerges, caesium auride, is not a metal alloy but plainly a salt: a semiconductor, close to transparent, and yellowish.

127
00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:38.470
Inside it, gold stands as a negatively charged ion.

128
00:12:38.470 --> 00:12:43.880
The only thing that makes this possible is the relativistically lowered energy of that electron.

129
00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:50.410
And one question becomes unavoidable: if 79 protons do this, what do 80 protons do?

130
00:12:50.410 --> 00:12:54.540
Immediately to the right of gold on the periodic table sits mercury.

131
00:12:54.540 --> 00:13:04.110
Its nucleus holds 80 protons, and unlike gold its outer shell is not half filled but completely filled; there is not a single electron there but a pair of them.

132
00:13:04.110 --> 00:13:09.520
Relativistic contraction pulls that pair toward the nucleus and renders it unusually stable.

133
00:13:09.520 --> 00:13:13.330
The result is a kind of chemical stinginess.

134
00:13:13.330 --> 00:13:18.420
Mercury atoms hold their own electrons tightly and will not share them with their neighbours.

135
00:13:18.420 --> 00:13:26.870
What makes metals hard and durable is that their atoms contribute generously to a common pool of electrons; in mercury that pool stays weak.

136
00:13:26.870 --> 00:13:32.760
The atoms cling to one another with a slackness better suited to the noble gases than to a metal.

137
00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:39.450
This is why mercury melts at minus 38.8 degrees Celsius and is the only metal that is liquid at room temperature.

138
00:13:39.450 --> 00:13:51.100
A computer simulation published in 2013 tested this directly: when relativistic effects were removed from the calculation, mercury's melting temperature shot up to 82 degrees Celsius.

139
00:13:51.100 --> 00:14:00.670
In other words, relativity pulls mercury's melting point down by about 105 degrees, and it does so passing precisely across room temperature on the way.

140
00:14:00.670 --> 00:14:06.720
Without relativity, mercury would be a grey metal you could pick up in your hand, just like lead.

141
00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:16.130
The barometer, the old thermometers, and the whole slippery, ungraspable image mercury summons in the human mind are all owed to those 105 degrees.

142
00:14:16.130 --> 00:14:20.580
Let us go one row further: 82 protons, lead.

143
00:14:20.580 --> 00:14:30.790
Lead's most familiar use is the battery discovered by Gaston Planté in 1859, which despite all the time that has passed since is still sitting under the bonnet of your car.

144
00:14:30.790 --> 00:14:39.240
A lead acid cell produces about 2.1 volts; you wire 6 cells in series and out comes the standard 12 volt battery.

145
00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:44.650
A cheap, reliable arrangement capable of meeting the enormous current demand of the starter motor.

146
00:14:44.650 --> 00:14:54.700
In 2011, researchers in Sweden and Finland calculated the voltage of that cell twice: once with equations that included relativity, and once without.

147
00:14:54.700 --> 00:14:56.910
The result was striking.

148
00:14:56.910 --> 00:15:05.680
Of the 2.1 volts per cell, roughly 1.7 volts — that is, about 80 percent — came directly from relativistic effects.

149
00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:11.570
Without relativity, the lead acid battery would never produce enough voltage to turn a starter motor.

150
00:15:11.570 --> 00:15:21.780
When you turn the key on a cold winter morning and hear the engine begin to spin, part of what is doing the work is electrons moving faster than half the speed of light at the bottom of a lead nucleus.

151
00:15:21.780 --> 00:15:27.830
This trail, which began with the yellow of gold, gave birth to an entire field in chemistry.

152
00:15:27.830 --> 00:15:36.760
Relativistic quantum chemistry is a mature discipline today; no serious calculation involving heavy elements is done without accounting for relativity.

153
00:15:36.760 --> 00:15:46.810
If you want to get the bond length of a molecule right, or why a catalyst works, or how the platinum inside a drug behaves, you have to put Einstein into the equation.

154
00:15:46.810 --> 00:15:56.220
At the very bottom of the table, among the artificial elements carrying more than 104 protons in their nuclei, the effect is expected to become still more dominant.

155
00:15:56.220 --> 00:16:04.350
The prediction is that in these elements the relativistic shifts grow so large that the habit of guessing an element's behaviour from the column it sits in may break down.

156
00:16:04.350 --> 00:16:08.960
This needs underlining: it is a prediction, not a settled result.

157
00:16:08.960 --> 00:16:17.570
The elements in question survive in the laboratory for fractions of a second, and their chemistry is tested one atom at a time, a few atoms per attempt.

158
00:16:17.570 --> 00:16:21.060
But the real point, I think, has to do with scale.

159
00:16:21.060 --> 00:16:25.830
The examples we are used to when relativity is explained are all far away.

160
00:16:25.830 --> 00:16:30.600
Atomic clocks on satellites drifting by a few microseconds a day.

161
00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:34.250
Starlight bending during a solar eclipse.

162
00:16:34.250 --> 00:16:39.020
Black holes, the universe, billions of years.

163
00:16:39.020 --> 00:16:46.350
All of it true, all of it measured, but none of it tangible; we accept every bit of it on the word of an instrument.

164
00:16:46.350 --> 00:16:48.880
The yellow of gold is not like that.

165
00:16:48.880 --> 00:16:54.450
It requires no instrument, no calculation, no intermediary.

166
00:16:54.450 --> 00:17:00.500
It is a colour looked at with the naked eye in broad daylight, and one that would not be there without relativity.

167
00:17:00.500 --> 00:17:09.270
Human beings have been gazing at it for more than 6,000 years; the oldest worked gold ever found dates from an age before writing itself existed.

168
00:17:09.270 --> 00:17:18.200
The two properties that make gold precious, that warm yellow and that stubborn refusal to tarnish, are, we now know, two faces of a single cause.

169
00:17:18.200 --> 00:17:23.290
Both are the work of electrons exceeding half the speed of light at the bottom of a nucleus.

170
00:17:23.290 --> 00:17:32.700
For thousands of years humanity placed gold in temples, in graves, and in crowns without knowing why it was the way it was, simply because it was.

171
00:17:32.700 --> 00:17:44.030
Then, in the twentieth century, a physicist thinking about moving bodies and the speed of light wrote down the equations, and it turned out that those equations, even in their most abstract form,

172
00:17:44.030 --> 00:17:47.360
had long been sitting on people's fingers.

173
00:17:47.360 --> 00:17:51.010
Nöron Science'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

174
00:17:51.010 --> 00:17:54.500
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
